president joe biden has confirmed that  he is going to go ahead and re-nominate  fed chairman jerome powell for a second  term as many of you already know the fed  chair is an appointed position it's not  an elected position that means that the  president and his administration is  responsible for going ahead and saying  this is who i want to go ahead and  propose to run the federal reserve the  central bank of the united states there  then is a confirmation process that they  go through in order to be confirmed as  that specific role now jerome powell was  appointed under president trump's  administration he's  the united states central bank over the  covid crisis as we know back in march of  2020 there was a liquidity crisis in  financial markets especially here in the  united states government ordered  everyone to go sit at home in their  homes there was uncertainty there was  fear and so what did people do they sold  off all of their financial assets when  they did that the dollar strength and  asset prices fell very very quickly we  saw public equities dropped 25 30 we saw  gold go down about 15 bitcoin went down  50 in a single day when you get a  liquidity crisis you can do one of two  things you can either choose not to  intervene you can allow the free market  to try to correct itself you can say hey  we're not going to step in here or the  central bank and say you know what we  have to intervene we have to mitigate  the short-term pain and that is exactly  what they did they issued two emergency  interest rate cuts all the way down to  zero percent interest rate environment  they flooded the market with cheap  capital they heavily incentivized people  to go borrow as much as they possibly  could and push that liquidity into the  market on top of that they had their  hand in providing quantitative easing  doing all sorts of different purchases  in financial markets the fed's balance  sheet since 2009 has gone from about 900  billion dollars to now over 8 trillion  dollars almost a 10 bagger in about 12  years and so ultimately what we saw was  all of that intervention all of that  financial stimulus that was pushed into  the market over the last 12 to 18 months  has given rise to one still over four  and a half percent unemployment in the  united states which is one of the  mandates of the federal reserve it's  full employment they have not met the  full employment on top of that the  second mandate is stable prices they  should keep prices stable but because we  are now living multiple months with over  five percent inflation last month's  inflation number was 6.2 percent on a  cpi basis the fed has failed at both  full employment and also stable prices  now their argument would be we had to do  it we had to mitigate the short-term  pain we had to flood the market with  lots and lots of liquidity in order to  make sure that americans were okay and  we made a direct trade-off between  mitigating short-term pain for the  medium to long-term pain that we would  have to address at a later date now the  problem is from the critics of the  federal reserve is that yes maybe you  had to do it maybe you didn't but we  told you that there was going to be high  levels of inflation you said that don't  worry about it there won't be and now we  have over six percent cpi it's the  official number the core inflation  number not including food and energy is  over four percent almost four and a half  percent and the unofficial number is in  the double digits and so ultimately what  you have to start to think about is that  essentially is a report card for the  chairman of the federal reserve now as i  have talked about over and over again in  the past i do not believe that who the  person is as the federal reserve  chairman would have mattered i don't  think that jerome powell did a good or  bad job i think he did exactly what he  had to do which is what anyone else in  that position would have been uh forced  to do regardless of the monetary policy  regardless of the theoretics around the  economic arguments this was much more of  a social argument we need help we need  help we need help bring help to the  people and so everyone from those who  had no clue what's going on all the way  up to the most sophisticated people  financial markets they were cheering on  the stimulus they wanted it they needed  it people were running to the government  saying please help me please help me and  the federal reserve was there to fill  that need both through the interest rate  manipulation down to zero and also the  quantitative easing so regardless of  whether it was your own power or anybody  else i think they would have done this  and so i do not think that you can  assign good or bad decision making on  the federal reserve chairman because  ultimately anyone would have done the  same thing in that position doesn't make  it right doesn't mean that it was in  hindsight the best decision but it does  mean that anyone would have done it  given the facts we had at the time so  ultimately there is probably no need to  replace him because he's doing the best  that he can now as i've talked about in  the past also is if the system is  designed in a way where they are going  to continue to do this they're going to  continue to be debase the currency  they're going to continue to manipulate  interest rates then you as an individual  investor complaining is irrelevant  complaining will not solve your problems  complaining is the mark of a loser  instead what you should do is you should  figure out how to invest regardless of  what financial markets you invest in  that is going to be your best path to  providing some level of financial  security for yourself and for your  family so whether you want to invest in  stock market whether you want to invest  in commodities whether you want to  invest in bitcoin or other crypto assets  learning how to invest is essential if  you want to protect your purchasing  power in today's economic environment so  ultimately regardless of the fed  chairman is you are going to have to  learn to invest don't worry about who's  in charge don't worry about what their  policies are because you i my brothers  and nobody else is going to change their  mind they're going to continue to do  what they do you just need to learn how  to invest so that you can position  yourself to benefit rather than be  punished by these decisions we've  undisciplined monetary and fiscal policy  in this country as do many countries  around the world that is because they  are backed into a corner they have no  other choice and so if you want to  benefit from that undisciplined decision  making then you need to be an investor  over the last 12 to 18 months the  wealthy the top 55 of americans who hold  investable assets they have gotten  richer and richer and richer they are  richer than they ever imagined because  they simply got long financial assets  and the currency was debased at a  historic rate and that pushed asset  prices up literally this morning the s p  500 hit another all-time high shocking  people love jerome powell they love the  money printer and they love the fact  that stocks continue to hit all-time  high after all-time high simply because  the currency is being debased now if you  are not one of the 55 percent of people  who hold investable assets and instead  you have zero investment assets to the  bottom 45 of americans you have been  punished over and over and over again  you are getting paid less money today  than you were 12 months ago and on top  of that you also have less purchasing  power in your savings than you did 12  months ago as well so you can't save you  have to invest and also you better be  asking for a big raise at work whether  you are an hourly wage worker or your  salaried worker you have to go in you  have to say inflation's at six percent  if i don't receive a six plus percent uh  raised then i literally am getting paid  less this